= Pareto principle — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''Pareto principle''', often called the '''80/20 rule''', is a rule of thumb suggesting that a relatively small share of causes, items or activities can account for a large share of a measured result. The exact 80% and 20% figures are '''not a universal law'''. Some distributions are highly concentrated; others are nearly uniform. An actual claim needs measurements and an explanation of what is being counted.<ref name="juran">Joseph M. Juran, [https://www.juran.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/The-Non-Pareto-Principle-1974.pdf ''The Non-Pareto Principle''] (1974), critical historical discussion of the principle's generalization beyond Pareto's wealth studies.</ref></onlyinclude>

== How does it work? ==

Imagine tracking 100 customer-service complaints. If 80 complaints relate to 20 of the 100 distinct causes, those 20 causes account for 80% of the observed complaints. Investigating them might be a productive way to prioritize work.

But if all 100 causes each produced one complaint, the largest 20 causes would account for only 20%. The 80/20 split would be wrong for those data.

These are invented examples illustrating how concentration depends on the measured distribution.

== Where did the name come from? ==

Economist Vilfredo Pareto studied unequal income distributions in the late nineteenth century. Quality engineer Joseph Juran later popularized applying the idea of the “vital few” to many practical problems. Juran himself wrote about the historical confusion between Pareto's observations and the subsequent broad generalization.<ref name="history">A. Tusset, [https://doi.org/10.1007/s12232-024-00458-7 ''Pareto and probability distributions''], historical research on Pareto's 1896–1897 income-distribution work; Juran (1974), [https://www.juran.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/The-Non-Pareto-Principle-1974.pdf ''The Non-Pareto Principle''].</ref>

The common story that Pareto discovered an exact universal 80/20 ratio is misleading.

== What is Pareto analysis? ==

In quality improvement, a team can count problems by category, sort categories from most frequent to least frequent, and display the result in a Pareto chart. The chart helps identify where measured problems are concentrated. It does not establish why each problem occurred.

A useful chart states the timeframe, unit of measurement, category definitions, completeness of data and what a change would accomplish.

== A caution about causes ==

A frequent problem category is not necessarily a cause. For example, slow page loading may be the most common complaint, while the physical cause could be a database bottleneck shared by many categories.

The chart is a starting point for investigation, not a substitute for [[Causality|causal reasoning]] or a controlled test.

== Other uses and mistakes ==

The same approach can examine manufacturing defects, workloads, revenue concentration or energy demand. But observations in one setting do not justify assuming the same ratio in another, and optimizing a single popular metric can neglect important rare failures.

The Pareto principle is also different from '''Pareto efficiency''' in economics: an option is Pareto efficient if improving someone's outcome necessarily worsens another's within the defined feasible alternatives. A concentration pattern and an efficiency criterion are different ideas.

